Theory‑Aware Memory Architecture

Toward Persistent Scientific Reasoning Beyond Context Windows

Ouadi Maakoul


Abstract

Large language models (LLMs) exhibit remarkable short‑term reasoning, yet their deployment in long‑term scientific projects is fundamentally limited by the absence of a persistent, logically coherent representation of an evolving theory. Current mitigation strategies—larger context windows, retrieval‑augmented generation (RAG), vector databases, and knowledge graphs—improve document lookup but do not maintain the logical integrity of a scientific theory whose definitions, lemmas, theorems, proofs, experiments, and conjectures continuously change. Empirical evidence now shows that reasoning quality degrades severely even with perfect retrieval, that full history causes unbounded memory growth, and that retrieval based on semantic similarity alone cannot capture the logical dependency structure that governs scientific discourse.

This dissertation proposes the Theory‑Aware Memory Architecture (TAMA), a new architectural layer situated between external memory and the language model. Rather than storing knowledge as text embeddings, TAMA represents scientific knowledge as a structured, typed dependency graph of logical objects. Every modification propagates through this graph via dependency analysis and automatic consistency verification before inference. The memory model is formalised as a consolidation operator \Phi that decomposes into insertion, propagation, and verification steps. We prove key properties: the uniqueness and minimality of the affected subgraph (Theorem 4.1), convergence of iterative consolidation to a stable theory via a Banach fixed‑point argument (Theorem 4.2), soundness of local consistency verification under acyclicity (Theorem 4.3), and computational complexity that depends only on the size of the affected subgraph, not the entire theory (Theorem 4.4). A trainable multi‑head system learns object classification, dependency prediction, contradiction detection, consolidation prediction, and theory evolution. Inference retrieves a logically closed subgraph rather than arbitrary paragraphs, ensuring that the language model receives a consistent context.

TAMA unifies recent empirical insights—LEDGER’s typed dependency graphs, CogMem’s hierarchical memory consolidation, LAG’s dependency‑aware grounding, and TheoremGraph’s statement‑level extraction—into a single formal framework. The work reframes memory in scientific AI from “which documents to retrieve?” to “which logical objects are affected by this change?”, thereby enabling persistent, self‑consistent reasoning across time scales that far exceed finite context windows. Because the dissertation is entirely theoretical, we provide a rigorous experimental design (Chapter 7) that specifies datasets, metrics, baseline comparisons, and ablation studies for future empirical validation.

