Chapter 1

Introduction

Large language models operate inside a finite context window. While the size of this window continues to grow, scientific research presents a fundamentally different challenge: a PhD dissertation, a long‑term engineering project, or a mathematical theory is not merely a long document. It is an evolving logical system in which a single change may have cascading consequences. Altering a definition can invalidate several lemmas; modifying a lemma can break multiple theorems; updating an experiment can shift theoretical assumptions; introducing new notation can create ambiguity throughout the manuscript.

Current solutions to the context limitation fall into two broad classes. Long‑context models attempt to extend the token capacity, but recent work has shown that reasoning quality degrades up to 85% even with perfect retrieval when context length grows unboundedly (CogMem, arXiv:2512.14118). Retrieval‑augmented generation stores documents or chunks in a vector database and retrieves them by semantic similarity. This approach improves fact recall but fails to capture the logical structure of a theory: retrieved chunks may be topically related yet logically inconsistent with the current state of the theory, and the system has no mechanism to detect or repair such inconsistencies. Even when retrieval is augmented with knowledge graphs, the graph typically serves as a static index, not as a living structure that evolves and verifies itself.

The central problem is therefore not memory capacity. It is the preservation of logical coherence during continuous theory evolution.

We propose the Theory‑Aware Memory Architecture (TAMA). TAMA is a new architectural layer that sits between external memory and the language model. It represents a scientific theory not as a collection of text chunks but as a typed dependency graph composed of scientific objects—axioms, definitions, lemmas, theorems, proofs, experiments, and conjectures. When a new piece of knowledge arrives, TAMA parses it into one or more objects, identifies their dependencies, updates the graph, propagates the effects of the change to all dependent objects, and verifies the consistency of the affected subgraph. Only objects that pass verification become active memory. At inference time, the language model does not receive a set of retrieved paragraphs; it receives a logically closed subgraph that is guaranteed to be internally consistent.

This dissertation provides the mathematical foundations of TAMA. We define the theory representation, the memory model, the consolidation operator, and the verification procedure. We prove the key properties of the architecture and connect them to empirical evidence from several recent systems. The work is purely theoretical; therefore, we include a detailed experimental plan (Chapter 7) to guide future empirical validation. By the end, we aim to have demonstrated that the core shift—from document retrieval to logical dependency maintenance—can serve as the foundation for persistent, self‑consistent machine reasoning.

