Chapter 2

Formal Foundations

2.1 Scientific Objects

Let \mathcal{O} be a countable universe of scientific objects. Each object o \in \mathcal{O} is a tuple

o = (\tau, s, \phi, \text{dep}, t, c)

where

· \tau \in \{\mathrm{A}, \mathrm{D}, \mathrm{N}, \mathrm{L}, \mathrm{Th}, \mathrm{P}, \mathrm{E}, \mathrm{C}, \mathrm{R}\} is the object type (axiom, definition, notation, lemma, theorem, proof, experiment, conjecture, reference);
· s \in \Sigma^* is the surface form (natural language text, possibly with formal notation);
· \phi \in \mathbb{R}^d is a learned embedding;
· \text{dep} \subseteq \mathcal{O} is a finite set of explicit dependencies;
· t \in \mathbb{R}^+ is a creation/modification timestamp;
· c \in [0,1] is a confidence score.

Certain types carry additional structured fields. Specifically,

\begin{aligned}
D_i &= (\mathrm{Name}_i, \mathrm{Content}_i, \mathrm{Dep}_i, \mathrm{Sig}_i), \quad \mathrm{Sig}_i \text{ a formal signature}\\
L_i &= (\mathrm{Stmt}_i, \mathrm{Proof}_i, \mathrm{Dep}_i)\\
Th_i &= (\mathrm{Stmt}_i, \mathrm{Proof}_i, \mathcal{L}_i, \mathcal{D}_i, \mathcal{A}_i)\\
E_i &= (\mathrm{Dataset}_i, \mathrm{Method}_i, \mathrm{Results}_i, \mathrm{Links}_i), \quad \mathrm{Links}_i \subseteq Th \cup L \cup D\\
C_i &= (\mathrm{Stmt}_i, \mathrm{Evid}_i, p_i), \quad p_i \in [0,1]
\end{aligned}

These refinements make the object schema rich enough to capture the logical structure of informal and formal scientific discourse, following the statement‑level model of TheoremGraph, which extracted 11.7 million statements and 18.3 million directed dependencies from a large corpus of informal mathematics (Kurgan et al., arXiv:2606.25363).

2.2 Theory as a Structured Tuple

A scientific theory at time t is defined as a tuple

\mathcal{T}_t = (A_t, D_t, N_t, L_t, Th_t, P_t, E_t, C_t, R_t)

where each component is a set of objects of the corresponding type. The carrier set is

V_t = A_t \cup D_t \cup N_t \cup L_t \cup Th_t \cup P_t \cup E_t \cup C_t \cup R_t.

Unlike a flat document collection, the theory is equipped with an explicit dependency structure.

2.3 Typed Dependency Graph

Definition 2.1 (Theory Graph). The theory graph G_t = (V_t, E_t, \lambda) is a directed labelled multigraph where

E_t \subseteq V_t \times V_t \times \mathcal{T}

and the edge types \mathcal{T} are drawn from

\{\texttt{REF}, \texttt{DEP}, \texttt{CONTAINS}, \texttt{RELATED}, \texttt{PROVES}, \texttt{REFUTES}, \texttt{USES}\}.

This set extends the four‑type model used in LEDGER (Wang et al., arXiv:2606.28379) with proof‑relation edges.

We distinguish two critical edge categories:

· Informational edges \texttt{REF}: v_i cites v_j; the connection is non‑logical.
· Logical dependency edges \texttt{DEP}: v_i counterfactually depends on v_j; if v_j were removed or changed, v_i would become ill‑defined or unjustified.

The reachability order \prec is defined by v_j \prec v_i iff there exists a directed path from v_j to v_i consisting only of \texttt{DEP} and \texttt{REF} edges.

Lemma 2.2 (Acyclicity Condition). If G_t contains a directed cycle comprising only \texttt{DEP} and \texttt{REF} edges, then the theory \mathcal{T}_t is ill‑founded.

Proof. Suppose a cycle v_1 \to v_2 \to \dots \to v_k \to v_1 exists. For k=1, an object depends on itself, which contradicts the principle that definitions must be grounded in previously established concepts. For k>1, the sequence of dependencies implies that the interpretation of v_1 requires that of v_k, which in turn requires that of v_1. No well‑founded definitional order can exist; the theory contains a circular justification. ∎

Consequently, TAMA enforces that the subgraph restricted to \texttt{DEP} \cup \texttt{REF} is a directed acyclic graph (DAG) and rejects any update that would introduce a cycle, using a depth‑first search of cost O(|V|+|E|).

Remark on mutual dependencies: Strict acyclicity can be too restrictive for legitimate scientific structures (e.g., simultaneous inductive definitions). A relaxation, left to future work, would introduce a co‑definition edge type and a stratification condition that allows cycles only if they do not preclude a well‑founded grounding order.

Lemma 2.3 (Deterministic Precision). The deterministic rule‑based dependency extractor used in TheoremGraph achieves 98.8% judge‑verified precision on informal mathematics. This justifies the use of pseudo‑labels y_{ij} from such an extractor to train the dependency prediction head \mathcal{L}_{dep} (see Chapter 5).

2.4 Embedding Space and Semantic Edges

The learned embedding \phi_i is produced by a function f_\theta trained such that \langle \phi_i, \phi_j \rangle approximates a logical entailment probability, not merely topical similarity. This is the central failure mode of vanilla RAG identified in Path‑Constrained Retrieval literature. Semantic edges (those with weight above a threshold \theta) can be added to capture inferred connections not explicitly stated; LEDGER uses \theta = 0.7 for such edges.

