Chapter 3

Theory‑Aware Memory Model

3.1 Memory Definition

Following the CogMem hierarchical memory architecture (Zhang et al., arXiv:2512.14118), TAMA’s memory is a three‑layer construct.

Definition 3.1 (Theory‑Aware Memory). At time t, the memory is a triple

M_t = (G_t, W_t, \Phi)

where

· G_t = (V_t, E_t, \lambda) is the typed dependency graph,
· W_t : E_t \to \mathbb{R}^+ assigns a learned weight to each edge,
· \Phi is the consolidation operator.

The full theory state is \mathcal{S}_t = (G_t, M_t, C_t), with C_t a vector of consistency metrics (logical consistency, contradiction rate, etc.).

The three layers correspond to CogMem’s memory types:

· Long‑Term Memory (LTM) — the persistent graph G_t,
· Direct Access (DA) — the active affected set \mathrm{Aff}(x_t) during an update,
· Focus of Attention (FoA) — the logically closed retrieved subgraph Sub(q) at inference time.

3.2 Memory Update

When new knowledge x_t (a raw text segment or a structured observation) arrives, the memory transitions according to

M_{t+1} = \Phi(M_t, x_t).

The consolidation operator decomposes into three sequential steps:

\Phi = \Phi_{\text{ver}} \circ \Phi_{\text{prop}} \circ \Phi_{\text{insert}}.

Step 1: Insertion \Phi_{\text{insert}}.
The incoming x_t is parsed into one or more scientific objects (see Chapter 5). Let O_{\text{new}} be the set of extracted objects and E_{\text{new}} the dependency edges among them and to existing objects. Then

G_t' = (V_t \cup O_{\text{new}}, \; E_t \cup E_{\text{new}}).

Step 2: Propagation \Phi_{\text{prop}}.
We compute the affected set \mathrm{Aff}(x_t) as the forward reachable nodes from any object in O_{\text{new}} through \texttt{DEP} edges:

\mathrm{Aff}(x_t) = \{ v \in V_t' \mid \exists u \in O_{\text{new}} \text{ with a path } u \leadsto v \text{ via } \texttt{DEP} \}.

The propagation step marks every node in \mathrm{Aff}(x_t) as potentially invalid and updates their dependency information. The weight function W_t is also adjusted: for each edge e incident to a node in \mathrm{Aff}(x_t), its weight is recomputed (or decayed) to reflect the uncertainty introduced by the change.
Remark: Not every dependent object necessarily becomes invalid; a theorem might remain true under a slightly modified lemma. In TAMA, we opt for a conservative (safe) propagation; a future refinement could introduce a change‑impact score that triggers re‑verification only above a threshold, thereby reducing computational load.

Step 3: Verification \Phi_{\text{ver}}.
Each node v \in \mathrm{Aff}(x_t) is subjected to a consistency check (Section 3.3). Nodes that fail are either repaired (if a repair procedure exists) or removed; edges are pruned accordingly. The resulting verified graph becomes G_{t+1}.

3.3 Consistency Verification

For a node v, the verification predicate is

\mathrm{Ver}(v) = \mathrm{Cons}(v) \land \mathrm{NoCirc}(v) \land \mathrm{NotRefute}(v) \land \mathrm{ProofComp}(v)

where

· \mathrm{Cons}(v): the conjunction of all statements in the dependency set \mathrm{dep}(v) is logically satisfiable. In practice, a SAT or SMT solver restricted to the decidable fragment of the dependency subgraph checks that \bigwedge_{u \in \mathrm{dep}(v)} u.\mathrm{stmt} \not\models \bot.
· \mathrm{NoCirc}(v): adding v does not create a \texttt{DEP}-cycle (enforced globally via DFS).
· \mathrm{NotRefute}(v): no conjecture or experiment in the theory directly refutes a theorem on which v depends.
· \mathrm{ProofComp}(v): if v is a theorem, every leaf of its proof tree is present in V_t.

Only nodes for which \mathrm{Ver}(v)=1 are retained in active memory. This local verification suffices for global consistency because the graph is a DAG and every node’s dependencies are themselves already verified (see Theorem 4.3).

Hybrid verification sketch: For the majority of informal statements, formal SAT/SMT is infeasible. TAMA is designed to accommodate a hybrid verifier: a symbolic core that handles statements expressed in a machine‑checkable fragment (e.g., Lean‑compatible signatures), coupled with an LLM‑based heuristic that assigns a confidence score c(v) to the consistency of the remaining dependencies. The formal soundness proof (Theorem 4.3) holds for the verifier’s internal accuracy; the practical system will degrade gracefully as the heuristic confidence drops.

