Chapter 4

Main Theorems

4.1 Minimal Affected Subgraph

Theorem 4.1 (Uniqueness and Minimality). For any update x_t that adds objects O_{\text{new}} to a consistent DAG G_t, the set \mathrm{Aff}(x_t) = \mathrm{Reach}^+_{G_t'}(O_{\text{new}}) is the unique \subseteq-minimal set of nodes such that G_t' \setminus \mathrm{Aff}(x_t) remains consistent.

Proof.
Soundness: If v \notin \mathrm{Aff}(x_t), there is no \texttt{DEP}-path from any u \in O_{\text{new}} to v. By definition of logical dependency, the interpretation [\![v]\!] = [\![v]\!]_{G_t \setminus O_{\text{new}}}, so v remains consistent.
Completeness: If v \in \mathrm{Aff}(x_t), there exists a chain u = v_0 \to v_1 \to \dots \to v_k = v of \texttt{DEP} edges. A counter‑model that changes the interpretation of v_0 will, by induction, change the interpretation of v_k; hence re‑verification is necessary.
Minimality: Any S' \subsetneq \mathrm{Aff}(x_t) misses some reachable node that may be inconsistent.
Uniqueness: Reachability in a finite directed graph is deterministic; breadth‑first search yields a unique set. ∎

This theorem formally justifies the shift from “which documents to retrieve?” to “which logical objects are affected?” and matches LEDGER’s empirical finding that dependency‑aware retrieval selects only the necessary context, keeping token usage constant per edit.

4.2 Convergence of Consolidation

Theorem 4.2 (Convergence). Let G_t' be a DAG with bounded edge weights.

1. The propagation step terminates in at most \mathrm{diam}(G_t') iterations.
2. If updates eventually cease and only weight decay is applied, the memory M_t converges to a unique stable theory M^*.

Proof.
(1) Since G_t' is a DAG, there exists a topological ordering \sigma: V \to \mathbb{N}. Propagation proceeds strictly along increasing \sigma; no node can be visited twice. Therefore the number of iterations is bounded by the length of the longest path, \mathrm{diam}(G_t') \le |V|-1.

(2) After insertions cease, V_{t+1}=V_t. Consider the metric space (\mathcal{W}, d_1) of edge weights with d_1(W,W') = \|W - W'\|_1, which is complete. Define the weight update operator T(W) = \gamma W + (1-\gamma)\hat{w}(x_t) with \gamma \in (0,1). Then

d_1(T(W), T(W')) = \|\gamma W + (1-\gamma)\hat{w} - \gamma W' - (1-\gamma)\hat{w}\|_1 = \gamma\|W-W'\|_1,

so T is a contraction. By the Banach fixed‑point theorem, there exists a unique fixed point W^* and W_t \to W^* exponentially: \|W_t - W^*\|_1 \le \gamma^t \|W_0 - W^*\|_1. Hence M_t \to M^* = (G, W^*, \Phi).

Corollary (Local stability without cessation): If updates never cease, each update affects only the bounded subgraph \mathrm{Aff}(x_t), so \|W_{t+1} - W_t\| is bounded by O(|\mathrm{Aff}|/|V|). The memory does not suffer from unbounded growth or catastrophic forgetting of unrelated parts.

4.3 Soundness of Consistency Verification

Theorem 4.3 (Soundness). Assume the verification predicate \mathrm{Ver}(v) is sound for each local check (i.e., \mathrm{Ver}(v)=1 \implies v is consistent with its dependencies). Then the theory \mathcal{T}_{t+1} obtained by applying \Phi_{\text{ver}} is globally logically consistent.

Proof. By induction on the topological order of G_{t+1}.
Base case: Axioms and objects with no dependencies are trivially consistent.
Inductive step: Assume for all u \prec v, \mathrm{Ver}(u)=1 and their knowledge base KB_u is consistent. \mathrm{Cons}(v) checks that KB_{\mathrm{dep}(v)} \cup \{v\} \not\models \bot; since all u \in \mathrm{dep}(v) are consistent by the induction hypothesis, this local check suffices. \mathrm{NoCirc} prevents cycles; \mathrm{NotRefute} and \mathrm{ProofComp} guarantee no counter‑evidence or missing proof steps. Hence v is consistent.

By induction, every node in the DAG is consistent, so the entire theory is consistent. The verification cost per edit is O(\sum_{v \in \mathrm{Aff}} \text{SAT}(|\mathrm{dep}(v)|)), not \text{SAT}(|V|).

Remark on checker soundness: The theorem relies on the idealised soundness of \mathrm{Cons}(v). In a real deployment, a hybrid verifier will produce a confidence rather than a Boolean. The overall consistency guarantee then degrades gracefully with the checker’s accuracy.

4.4 Computational Complexity

Theorem 4.4 (Complexity). Let n = |V_t|, m = |E_t|, and a = |\mathrm{Aff}(x_t)| for a given update.

· Dependency extraction: O(a \cdot L_{\mathrm{LM}}), where L_{\mathrm{LM}} is the cost of the LLM‑based parser.
· Propagation: O(a + e_a), where e_a is the number of edges in the induced subgraph on \mathrm{Aff}(x_t).
· Retrieval for inference: the size of the retrieved subgraph depends only on the query scope, not on n; retrieval time is O(1) with respect to theory growth.

Proof. Extraction runs the parser on the incoming text and on the text of each affected node to re‑compute dependencies; this is linear in a. Propagation traverses the forward reachable set, which is O(a + e_a). Retrieval constructs a relevant subgraph Sub(q) by taking the downward closure of top‑k nodes; its size is bounded by a constant (determined by branching factor and depth bound), independent of n. ∎

This property is crucial for multi‑year projects: the system does not slow down as the theory grows. LEDGER empirically validates this: it maintains a constant ~1,500 tokens per edit regardless of document size, an 85% reduction compared to full regeneration.

