Chapter 5

Learning and Multi‑Agent Architecture

5.1 Training Objectives

TAMA is not solely a static algorithm; its components are trained to improve extraction, dependency prediction, and consistency verification. Following the Logic‑Augmented Generation (LAG) paradigm (Xiao et al., arXiv:2508.05509), we define five training heads:

1. Object Classification \mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}: minimise -\log P(\tau \mid s), the log‑likelihood of the object type given its surface form.
2. Dependency Prediction \mathcal{L}_{\text{dep}}: for each pair of objects i,j, predict the presence of a dependency edge. The loss is
   \mathcal{L}_{\text{dep}} = -\sum_{i,j} \big[ y_{ij}\log \sigma(w_{ij}^\top [\phi_i;\phi_j]) + (1-y_{ij})\log(1-\sigma(\dots)) \big],
   \]  
   where y_{ij} are pseudo‑labels from the deterministic extractor (TheoremGraph’s 98.8% precision) and w_{ij} is an edge‑type‑specific weight vector.
3. Contradiction Detection \mathcal{L}_{\text{contr}}: a contrastive loss
   \mathcal{L}_{\text{contr}} = \max(0, \mu - d(\phi_{Th}, \phi_{\neg Th})),
   \]  
   where \phi_{\neg Th} is the embedding of a synthetically negated statement.
4. Consolidation Prediction \mathcal{L}_{\text{consol}}: predicts the affected set \mathrm{Aff}(x_t) directly, trained on simulated theory edits.
5. Theory Evolution Prediction \mathcal{L}_{\text{evol}}: a graph‑generation objective P(G_{t+1} \mid G_t, x_t), encouraging the system to anticipate how a theory will change.

These objectives are optimised jointly, creating a second learning process that runs alongside the base language model. The dependency prediction head effectively implements LAG’s principle of decomposing complex queries into atomic sub‑questions ordered by logical dependencies.

5.2 Multi‑Agent Internal Pipeline

The architecture is implemented as a collection of specialised agents that execute the pipeline sequentially:

· Parser Agent: receives raw user input and extracts objects with types, surface forms, and candidate dependencies.
· Graph Agent: integrates the extracted objects into the existing dependency graph, checks for cycles, and proposes initial edge weights.
· Proof Agent: for theorems and lemmas, verifies the proof structure and completion.
· Consistency Agent: runs the SAT/SMT‑based verifier (or its hybrid counterpart) on the affected subgraph.
· Citation Agent: validates external references and their status.
· Retriever Agent: at inference time, computes the relevant subgraph using Personalised PageRank with query‑specific priors.
· Generator Agent: the language model itself, which receives the consistent subgraph and produces the final response.

Agents communicate through a shared blackboard (the theory state \mathcal{S}_t). This modular design allows independent improvement of each component and mirrors CogMem’s hierarchical consolidation loops.

