Chapter 6

Inference as Subgraph Retrieval

Unlike classical RAG, which retrieves a set of document chunks by vector similarity, TAMA retrieves a logically closed subgraph.

Definition 6.1 (Retrieved Subgraph). For a query q, let the relevance of a node v be

\text{Rel}(v,q) = \alpha \cdot \text{sim}(\phi_q, \phi_v) + (1-\alpha) \cdot \text{PPR}_{G_t}(v \mid q),

where \text{PPR} is the Personalised PageRank score with a teleport vector concentrated on nodes that match the query. The system selects the top‑k most relevant nodes and then constructs

Sub(q) = \{ v \mid v \text{ is selected or } v \text{ is a } \texttt{DEP}\text{-ancestor of a selected node} \}.

This set satisfies downward closure: if v \in Sub(q) then \mathrm{dep}(v) \subseteq Sub(q). Thus the language model receives a self‑contained, logically coherent context. The size of Sub(q) is bounded by k \cdot b^{d}, where b is the average branching factor and d is the maximum dependency depth; both are independent of the total theory size |V_t|.

Inference then proceeds as

y = f_\theta(x, Sub(q)),

instead of y = f_\theta(x, \text{top-}k\text{ chunks}). This shift addresses a known failure mode: vector similarity alone misses structural dependencies that are critical for multi‑step reasoning.

