Chapter 7

Experimental Design and Future Validation

Because this dissertation is a theoretical contribution, we present a rigorous experimental framework to guide future empirical validation. The following design allows a direct comparison of TAMA against baselines on the tasks of long‑term theory maintenance and consistent inference.

7.1 Datasets

· Primary corpus: The TheoremGraph dataset (Kurgan et al., 2026), containing 11.7 million statements and 18.3 million directed dependencies extracted from informal mathematical texts. This provides a realistic, large‑scale graph with pre‑existing dependency labels and a deterministic extraction pipeline of known precision (98.8%).
· Synthetic theory edits: We will generate a suite of controlled edits—definition changes, lemma modifications, new theorem additions, and experimental updates—simulating the evolution of a scientific manuscript over time. Each edit is paired with a ground‑truth affected set, validated by domain experts for a subset of 500 edits.

7.2 Baselines

1. Vanilla LLM with a large context window (e.g., 128k tokens), fed the full accumulated text.
2. Standard RAG pipeline: chunked embeddings + top‑k retrieval by cosine similarity.
3. Knowledge‑Graph‑Augmented RAG (KG‑RAG): a static knowledge graph used as an index, without consistency propagation.
4. LEDGER‑only (Wang et al., 2026): the typed graph retrieval system without propagation/verification, to isolate the contribution of the full consolidation operator.

7.3 Evaluation Metrics

· Logical Consistency (LC): LC = 1 - \frac{|\{v \in V_t \mid \neg \mathrm{Ver}(v)\}|}{|V_t|}
· Theorem Recall (TR): TR = \frac{|Th_{\text{retrieved}} \cap Th_{\text{relevant}}|}{|Th_{\text{relevant}}|}
· Definition Stability (DS): DS = \frac{1}{|D|} \sum_{D_i} e^{-\|\phi(D_i^t) - \phi(D_i^{t-1})\|}
· Notation Stability (analogous)
· Contradiction Rate (CR): CR = \frac{|\{(v_i,v_j) \mid v_i \models \neg v_j\}|}{|V|^2}
· Proof Preservation (PP): PP = \frac{|\{Th \mid \text{Proof}_{t+1}(Th) \vdash_t Th\}|}{|Th|}
· Memory Efficiency: total storage cost relative to the flat text baseline.
· Latency: end‑to‑end response time, decomposed into extraction, propagation, verification, and generation.

7.4 Ablation Studies

We will ablate: (a) the propagation step (i.e., static graph vs. dynamic propagation), (b) the verification module (full vs. no consistency check), (c) the dependency prediction head (using only deterministic edges vs. learned edges), and (d) the subgraph closure strategy (downward closure vs. simple top‑k).

7.5 Implementation Plan

A prototype will be built using:

· Parser: A fine‑tuned T5‑large model for statement extraction and typing, bootstrapped with TheoremGraph’s rule‑based labels.
· Graph database: Neo4j for storing G_t and running reachability queries.
· Verifier: A hybrid pipeline: Z3 for statements that can be automatically formalised; a GPT‑4‑based heuristic for informal statements, outputting a confidence score that is thresholded.
· Learning heads: Implemented in PyTorch, trained jointly with a frozen LLM base.

The primary experiment will run 100 sequential edits on a theory of initially 10,000 objects, measuring all metrics at each step. We expect TAMA to maintain significantly higher Logical Consistency and Proof Preservation than baselines, with Definition Stability approaching 1.0 after initial transients.

