Chapter 8

Related Work

TAMA integrates ideas from several recent lines of research.

Hierarchical Memory for LLMs. CogMem (Zhang et al., 2025) proposes a three‑layer memory (working, episodic, semantic) with consolidation loops. TAMA adopts this hierarchical view but specialises the semantic layer to a typed dependency graph, making it theory‑aware.

Dependency‑Aware Retrieval. LEDGER (Wang et al., 2026) shows that explicit typed dependency graphs improve consistency from 56% to 76% across models while keeping retrieval cost constant per edit. TAMA generalises LEDGER’s static graph into a dynamic memory with propagation and verification, and provides the formal underpinnings.

Logic‑Augmented Generation. LAG (Xiao et al., 2025) reframes augmentation as dependency‑aware decomposition and sequential grounding, reducing hallucination. TAMA’s inference‑time subgraph retrieval with downward closure is a direct realisation of LAG’s principles.

Statement‑Level Graph Extraction. TheoremGraph (Kurgan et al., 2026) demonstrates that informal mathematics can be automatically parsed into a directed graph of 11.7M statements and 18.3M dependencies with high precision. This provides the parser backbone for TAMA and validates the feasibility of large‑scale object extraction.

Neuro‑Symbolic and Proof‑Assistant Integration. Systems such as Lean Copilot and GPT‑f have combined LLMs with formal proof assistants. TAMA can be seen as a bridge: its graph structure is compatible with kernel‑level dependencies in Lean, enabling eventual formal verification.

Earlier Structured Memory Models. Neural Turing Machines, Memory Networks, and more recent graph‑retrieval systems (REALM, Atlas) addressed structured memory, but none dynamically maintained logical consistency under theory evolution. TAMA’s consolidation operator is the differentiating contribution.

