Chapter 9

Discussion and Future Directions

Limitations. The current formulation assumes high‑quality extraction of objects and dependencies; in practice, extraction errors will propagate. The confidence score c on objects provides a partial remedy, but a full treatment of probabilistic dependency edges and uncertain reasoning is left for future work. The verification step relies on a decidable logical fragment, which may not capture all nuances of natural scientific language. The hybrid verifier sketched in Chapter 3 is a first step toward addressing this.

Relaxing Acyclicity. The strict DAG requirement may reject legitimate co‑definitions (e.g., mutual inductive types). A future extension could allow cycles that are well‑founded under a stratification ordering, preserving the soundness of local verification while supporting richer theory structures.

Category‑Theoretic Semantics. One can view the set of all consistent theories as a category \mathbf{Th} where morphisms are updates that preserve \texttt{DEP}-closure. The consolidation operator \Phi is an endofunctor, and consistency verification acts as a subobject classifier selecting consistent subtheories. While this observation is not yet fully developed, it suggests deep connections with formal verification and could guide the design of self‑healing graphs that repair inconsistencies automatically.

Multi‑Agent Collaboration. The current architecture is single‑user; extending TAMA to a shared, distributed memory that multiple AI agents (and humans) can collaboratively evolve would be a natural next step, requiring merge and conflict‑resolution operators.

Continual Learning. Theorem 4.2 guarantees convergence only when updates cease. In lifelong learning settings, new mechanisms are needed to prevent catastrophic forgetting while allowing indefinite theory growth. Modular memory and elastic weight consolidation are possible approaches.

Autonomous Discovery. With a persistent, self‑consistent theory memory, an AI system could autonomously formulate conjectures, design experiments, and refine theories over weeks or months. TAMA provides the representational foundation for such autonomous scientific agents.

Experimental Roadmap. Chapter 7 details the immediate next steps: implementing the prototype and running the proposed experiments. Beyond that, we envision deployment in a real‑world scientific collaboration, where the system assists a team of researchers in maintaining a living document, automatically flagging inconsistencies and suggesting repairs.

