Chapter 10

Conclusion

This dissertation has presented the Theory‑Aware Memory Architecture (TAMA), a new memory layer for language models that represents scientific knowledge as a typed, evolving dependency graph. We have formally defined the theory representation, the memory model, the consolidation operator, and the consistency verification process. The main theorems establish the minimality of the affected subgraph, the convergence of consolidation to a stable state (via Banach’s fixed‑point theorem), the soundness of local verification, and computational efficiency that scales with the size of the change rather than the entire theory.

TAMA’s key insight is the transformation of memory retrieval into dependency computation. Instead of asking “which documents are similar to the query?”, the system asks “which logical objects are affected by this change?” and “which subgraph is needed to make this inference consistent?”. By maintaining the logical architecture of knowledge itself, rather than its textual surface, TAMA enables language models to participate in scientific projects that extend over months or years while preserving mathematical consistency.

The work unifies disparate empirical successes—LEDGER’s typed graphs, CogMem’s hierarchical consolidation, LAG’s dependency‑aware grounding, TheoremGraph’s large‑scale extraction—into a single formal framework. It lays the groundwork for a new generation of persistent, self‑consistent reasoning machines. Future intelligent systems may be judged not by the number of tokens they can process, but by how faithfully they can preserve, extend, and verify the internal structure of human knowledge.


