Appendix A – Full Proofs and Formal Details

A.1 Detailed Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Minimal Affected Subgraph)

Theorem 4.1. Let G_t = (V_t, E_t) be a consistent DAG. For an update x_t adding objects O_{\text{new}} and edges E_{\text{new}}, define G_t' = (V_t \cup O_{\text{new}}, E_t \cup E_{\text{new}}) and \mathrm{Aff}(O_{\text{new}}) = \{ v \in V_t' \mid \exists u \in O_{\text{new}} \text{ with a } \texttt{DEP}\text{-path } u \leadsto v \}. Then \mathrm{Aff}(O_{\text{new}}) is the unique \subseteq-minimal set of vertices such that G_t' \setminus \mathrm{Aff}(O_{\text{new}}) is consistent with respect to the pre‑update theory.

Proof.

1. Soundness (sufficiency of \mathrm{Aff}). Take any v \notin \mathrm{Aff}(O_{\text{new}}). By definition, there is no \texttt{DEP}-path from any node in O_{\text{new}} to v. The interpretation [\![v]\!] in the theory depends only on its \texttt{DEP}-ancestors. Since none of those ancestors can be in O_{\text{new}} (otherwise a path would exist), the set of ancestors of v is unchanged from G_t. Therefore the truth value of v is unaffected by the new objects; v remains consistent with the original theory. Hence G_t' \setminus \mathrm{Aff}(O_{\text{new}}) is consistent.
2. Completeness (necessity of \mathrm{Aff}). Let v \in \mathrm{Aff}(O_{\text{new}}). Then there exists a chain u = v_0 \xrightarrow{\texttt{DEP}} v_1 \xrightarrow{\texttt{DEP}} \dots \xrightarrow{\texttt{DEP}} v_k = v with u \in O_{\text{new}}. Consider a model where the interpretation of u is altered (e.g., from true to false). By the counterfactual definition of \texttt{DEP}, the interpretation of v_1 could change, then v_2, and by induction v could change. Thus the validity of v is not guaranteed without re‑verification; it cannot be assumed consistent.
3. Minimality. Suppose there exists S \subsetneq \mathrm{Aff}(O_{\text{new}}) that is also sufficient. Then there is some v \in \mathrm{Aff}(O_{\text{new}}) \setminus S. Since v is reachable, the argument of completeness shows that excluding v risks inconsistency. Hence no proper subset can suffice; \mathrm{Aff} is minimal.
4. Uniqueness. The definition of \mathrm{Aff}(O_{\text{new}}) is purely graph‑theoretic: forward reachability in a finite directed graph. Algorithmically, breadth‑first search from O_{\text{new}} computes exactly this set deterministically. Therefore there is exactly one such set. ∎

A.2 Detailed Proof of Theorem 4.2 (Convergence)

Theorem 4.2. Let G_t' be a DAG and W_t bounded. The consolidation procedure satisfies:

1. The propagation step terminates after at most \mathrm{diam}(G_t') iterations.
2. If, after a finite time, no further insertions/deletions occur and only weight decay is applied, the memory M_t converges to a unique stable state M^*.

Proof of (1). Because G_t' is a DAG, we can assign a topological ordering \sigma : V \to \mathbb{N} such that for every edge (u,v), \sigma(u) < \sigma(v). In the propagation step, when a node is updated, only its successors can be queued for re‑evaluation. Therefore, any propagation chain follows strictly increasing \sigma. No node can be processed twice in the same propagation wave because its status stabilises once all predecessors have been processed. Consequently, the number of iterations is bounded by the length of the longest path in the DAG, i.e., the diameter \mathrm{diam}(G_t') \le |V|-1. Termination is guaranteed. ∎

Proof of (2). After insertions and deletions have ceased, the vertex set V becomes fixed. The only remaining change is the weight update rule

W_{t+1}(e) = \gamma W_t(e) + (1-\gamma) \hat{w}_t(e),

with \gamma \in (0,1), where \hat{w}_t(e) is a bounded adjustment (e.g., re‑estimated weight after a local change; once changes stop, \hat{w}_t(e) becomes constant). We work in the Banach space (\mathbb{R}^{|E|}, \|\cdot\|_1) of edge weight vectors, which is complete. Define the operator T: \mathbb{R}^{|E|} \to \mathbb{R}^{|E|} by T(W) = \gamma W + (1-\gamma) \mathbf{c}, where \mathbf{c} is the constant vector of post‑change weights. For any two weight vectors W, W',

\|T(W) - T(W')\|_1 = \|\gamma (W - W')\|_1 = \gamma \|W - W'\|_1.

Since \gamma \in (0,1), T is a strict contraction. By the Banach fixed‑point theorem, there exists a unique W^* such that T(W^*) = W^*, and for any initial W_0, the sequence W_{t+1} = T(W_t) converges to W^* at an exponential rate: \|W_t - W^*\|_1 \le \gamma^t \|W_0 - W^*\|_1. Hence M_t = (G, W_t, \Phi) converges to M^* = (G, W^*, \Phi). ∎

A.3 Detailed Proof of Theorem 4.3 (Soundness of Local Verification)

Theorem 4.3. Assume that the verification predicate \mathrm{Ver}(v) is locally sound: \mathrm{Ver}(v) = 1 implies that v is logically consistent with its immediate dependencies. If the theory graph is a DAG, then applying \Phi_{\text{ver}} yields a globally consistent theory \mathcal{T}_{t+1}.

Proof. By induction on the topological order of the final graph G_{t+1}.

Base case. Nodes with \mathrm{dep}(v) = \emptyset (axioms, raw data) are verified trivially: \mathrm{Cons}(v) vacuously holds, and there are no cycles or missing proofs. They are consistent.

Inductive hypothesis. Assume for all u with \sigma(u) < \sigma(v) that \mathrm{Ver}(u) = 1 and that the set of all such u forms a consistent subtheory.

Inductive step. Consider v. Its dependencies \mathrm{dep}(v) all have topological order strictly less than \sigma(v) (otherwise a cycle would exist). Hence, by the inductive hypothesis, the conjunction KB_{\mathrm{dep}(v)} = \bigwedge_{u \in \mathrm{dep}(v)} u.\mathrm{stmt} is satisfiable and consistent. The verification predicate checks:

· \mathrm{Cons}(v): KB_{\mathrm{dep}(v)} \land v.\mathrm{stmt} is satisfiable. Since the background KB is consistent, adding v preserves consistency.
· \mathrm{NoCirc}(v): adding v introduces no \texttt{DEP}-cycle; the graph remains a DAG.
· \mathrm{NotRefute}(v): no statement in the theory refutes a theorem on which v depends; this is a semantic check that can be reduced to testing that no conjecture E with p > 1-\epsilon is inconsistent with v’s dependencies.
· \mathrm{ProofComp}(v): if v is a theorem, all proof leaves are present and verified.

Thus v is consistent with all earlier nodes. By induction, every node in G_{t+1} is consistent with its predecessors, so the whole theory is consistent. ∎

A.4 Detailed Proof of Theorem 4.4 (Complexity)

Theorem 4.4. Let n = |V_t|, m = |E_t|, a = |\mathrm{Aff}(x_t)|, and L_{\mathrm{LM}} be the cost of the parser. Then:

· Extraction: O(a \cdot L_{\mathrm{LM}}).
· Propagation: O(a + e_a), where e_a is the number of edges in the induced subgraph on \mathrm{Aff}(x_t).
· Retrieval: the size of the retrieved subgraph Sub(q) is independent of n; retrieval time is O(1) with respect to theory growth.

Proof. Extraction runs the LLM‑based parser on the incoming text and on the text of each node in \mathrm{Aff}(x_t) to re‑compute its dependencies and embedding. As there are at most a such nodes, the cost is bounded by a \cdot L_{\mathrm{LM}}.

Propagation: The affected set is computed by forward reachability from O_{\text{new}} along \texttt{DEP} edges. This can be implemented via BFS/DFS in time O(a + e_a), where e_a is the number of edges incident to nodes in \mathrm{Aff}(x_t). Each such edge is visited at most once.

Retrieval: Sub(q) is built by first selecting the top‑k nodes according to a relevance score, then taking the downward closure under \texttt{DEP}. The size of the closure is bounded by k \cdot b^d, where b is the average branching factor and d the maximal dependency depth. Both b and d are structural properties of the theory, independent of the overall theory size n. Thus |Sub(q)| = O(1) relative to n, and the retrieval time is constant. ∎

A.5 Lemmas

Lemma 2.2 (Acyclicity Condition). If a cycle of \texttt{DEP} \cup \texttt{REF} exists, the theory is ill‑founded.

Proof. Let the cycle be v_1 \to v_2 \to \dots \to v_k \to v_1. For k=1, an object depends on itself, violating the well‑foundedness of definitions. For k>1, the chain yields a cyclic dependency: the interpretation of v_1 requires that of v_k, which requires that of v_1. No grounding order exists. ∎

Lemma 2.3 (Deterministic Precision). The deterministic rule‑based extractor of TheoremGraph achieves 98.8% judge‑verified precision on informal mathematical text, justifying its use to generate training labels y_{ij} for the dependency prediction head.

