Appendix C – Learning Objectives and Training Details

C.1 Loss Functions

· Object Classification: \mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}} = -\frac{1}{N}\sum_i \log P(\tau_i \mid s_i; \theta_{\text{cls}}), where \tau_i is the true type.
· Dependency Prediction: For each pair (i,j) with pseudo‑label y_{ij} from the deterministic extractor,
  \mathcal{L}_{\text{dep}} = -\sum_{i,j} \left[ y_{ij} \log \sigma(w_{ij}^\top [\phi_i;\phi_j]) + (1-y_{ij}) \log(1-\sigma(\dots)) \right].
· Contradiction Detection: Contrastive margin loss:
  \mathcal{L}_{\text{contr}} = \max(0, \mu - \|\phi_{Th} - \phi_{\neg Th}\|_2),
  where \phi_{\neg Th} is the embedding of a synthetically negated statement generated by a fixed set of templates.
· Consolidation Prediction: A binary classification head over nodes v predicting whether v \in \mathrm{Aff}(x_t); trained on simulated edits with ground‑truth affected sets derived by exhaustive reachability.
· Theory Evolution Prediction: A graph generative loss: maximize P(G_{t+1} \mid G_t, x_t) using a Graph Neural Network that predicts added edges and nodes.

These objectives are jointly minimized: \mathcal{L}_{\text{total}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}} + \lambda_1 \mathcal{L}_{\text{dep}} + \lambda_2 \mathcal{L}_{\text{contr}} + \lambda_3 \mathcal{L}_{\text{consol}} + \lambda_4 \mathcal{L}_{\text{evol}}, with hyperparameters tuned on a validation set of theory edits.

C.2 Pseudo‑label Generation

We adopt the TheoremGraph deterministic pipeline: regular expression patterns for common mathematical constructs, cross‑reference resolution, and scoping rules that identify logical dependency. The resulting edges have a verified precision of 98.8%, making them reliable supervision for the dependency prediction head. In the future, this head could be fine‑tuned with human‑annotated dependencies in specialised domains.

C.3 Training Procedure

The system is trained in two phases:

1. Pre‑training: The parser and embedding models are pre‑trained on a large corpus of scientific papers to recognise object types and surface forms.
2. Fine‑tuning with simulated theory evolution: A series of synthetic theories (each a DAG of ~500 objects) is perturbed by random edits (definition change, lemma insertion, etc.). The full TAMA pipeline is rolled out, and all five losses are back‑propagated through the differentiable components. The graph operations (reachability, SAT calls) are non‑differentiable; their outcomes are treated as fixed targets for the trainable heads.

